---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import PipelineDiagram from '@/components/PipelineDiagram.astro'

const destinations = `{
  "forward": [
    {
      "vendor": "langfuse",
      "region": "eu",
      "public_key": "\${LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY}",
      "secret_key": "\${LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY}",
      "filter": { "projects": ["checkout"], "sample": 0.25 },
      "redact": { "key_contains": ["email"] }
    },
    {
      "name": "honeycomb-prod",
      "vendor": "honeycomb",
      "api_key": "\${HONEYCOMB_API_KEY}",
      "dataset": "agents",
      "signals": { "traces": true, "logs": true, "metrics": false }
    },
    {
      "name": "collector",
      "endpoint": "https://otel.internal.example.com:4318",
      "protocol": "protobuf",
      "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer \${COLLECTOR_TOKEN}" },
      "redact": { "drop_payloads": true }
    }
  ]
}`
---

<Doc
  title="Forwarding"
  description="Copy what mcpd stores on to one or more destinations: Langfuse, LangSmith, Braintrust, Arize, Phoenix, Datadog, Honeycomb, New Relic, Grafana, Sentry, Weave, Laminar, Helicone, Opik, Elastic, Dynatrace, or any OTLP receiver. Local storage always happens first, and each copy waits in its own spool until the destination takes it."
  lede="mcpd speaks OTLP in both directions. Nothing that arrives is stuck here."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>One store, as many copies as you need</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd receives OTLP, and it sends OTLP. A destination is one entry in the
    config file: a vendor name, a credential, and optionally a filter, a
    redaction rule and the signals it takes. mcpd knows the endpoint, the
    authentication header and the attribute names each vendor reads, so you
    write the key and not the integration.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also means adopting mcpd is reversible. Point it at whatever you were
    using before, run both for a fortnight, and decide with the data in front of
    you rather than on a migration you cannot undo.
  </p>

  <h2>Turning it on</h2>
  <p>
    For one OTLP receiver, the flags are enough. Give the base URL and not
    <code>/v1/traces</code>: the path of each signal is appended.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="store locally and copy onward"
    command="mcpd serve --forward-endpoint https://otlp.example.com"
    primaryLabel="OTLP"
    secondary={[
      {
        label: 'With a header',
        command: "mcpd serve --forward-endpoint https://otlp.example.com --forward-header 'Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN'",
      },
      {
        label: 'A vendor',
        command: "mcpd serve --forward-vendor honeycomb --forward-header 'x-honeycomb-team: $HONEYCOMB_API_KEY'",
      },
      {
        label: 'Protobuf',
        command: 'mcpd serve --forward-endpoint https://otlp.example.com --forward-protocol protobuf',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    <code>--forward-vendor</code> picks a vendor's endpoint and conventions;
    the credential goes in <code>--forward-header</code>. With a vendor that has
    a default endpoint, <code>--forward-endpoint</code> may be left out.
    <code>--forward-protocol</code> takes <code>json</code>,
    <code>protobuf</code> or <code>grpc</code>. A destination given by flags
    replaces the file's <code>forward</code> block, so one instance can be
    pointed elsewhere without editing a shared ConfigMap.
  </p>

  <h2>Several destinations</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>forward</code> block in the config file takes one destination as
    an object, which is the form earlier releases read and still works, or an
    array of them. Each destination gets its own copy, its own spool and its
    own retries, so a slow vendor never holds back a fast one.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{destinations}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Any string may be written <code>$&#123;ENV_NAME&#125;</code>. It is read
    from the environment once, at start. A variable that is not set refuses
    the start and names the variable, so a credential missing from a secret is
    an error you see at deploy time and not a 401 you find a week later.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>name</code></td>
          <td>The destination's name, used as the <code>destination</code> label on its metrics and as its spool directory. Letters, digits, <code>-</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>.</code>. Defaults to the vendor name</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>vendor</code></td>
          <td>One of the presets below. Defaults to <code>otlp</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>endpoint</code></td>
          <td>The base URL, without <code>/v1/traces</code>. Required for <code>otlp</code>, <code>otlp_grpc</code>, <code>elastic</code> and <code>dynatrace</code>; for a vendor with a default, it replaces that default</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>protocol</code></td>
          <td><code>json</code>, <code>protobuf</code> or <code>grpc</code>. <code>otlp</code> defaults to JSON, <code>otlp_grpc</code> to gRPC, every other vendor to protobuf. Dynatrace refuses JSON</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>headers</code></td>
          <td>A list of <code>[name, value]</code> pairs or an object. A header with the same name as one the preset sets replaces it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>enabled</code></td>
          <td><code>false</code> keeps the entry in the file and sends nothing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>batch_size</code></td>
          <td>Spans per request. Default 256</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>conventions</code></td>
          <td>The attribute names the copy is written in: <code>gen_ai</code>, <code>gen_ai_messages</code>, <code>openinference</code>, <code>langfuse</code>, <code>langsmith</code>, <code>braintrust</code> or <code>laminar</code>. Defaults to the vendor's own</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>region</code></td>
          <td>Picks the vendor's regional endpoint, listed in the table below</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>api_key</code>, <code>token</code>, <code>public_key</code>, <code>secret_key</code>, <code>instance_id</code>, <code>space_id</code></td>
          <td>Credentials. Each vendor reads the ones the table names and turns them into its own header</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>project</code>, <code>workspace</code>, <code>dataset</code>, <code>dsn</code></td>
          <td>Where in the vendor the copy lands</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>filter</code></td>
          <td>Which data this destination receives. See below</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>redact</code></td>
          <td>What this destination's copy leaves out. See below</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>signals</code></td>
          <td><code>traces</code>, <code>logs</code> and <code>metrics</code>, each true or false. Defaults to every signal the vendor accepts</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>spool_max_bytes</code></td>
          <td>The most this destination's spool may hold. Defaults to <code>--spool-max-bytes</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

