  <h2>What every copy carries</h2>
  <p>
    Whichever conventions a destination uses, the copy also carries the
    <code>gen_ai.*</code> attributes: the operation name, the request model,
    the system and provider name, input and output tokens, the conversation id,
    the agent and tool names, and the request's temperature, top_p and
    max_tokens. It carries <code>session.id</code> and <code>user.id</code>,
    which is already the hash when mcpd hashes it. And it carries the
    <code>mcpd.*</code> attributes, the span kind, entity name, cost and tags,
    with <code>mcpd.project</code> on the resource. A copy sent to another mcpd
    therefore lands unchanged, in the same project.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spans keep their links, with the link kind as <code>mcpd.link.kind</code>,
    and their events: an event span goes back onto its carrier as an OTLP span
    event when the carrier is in the same batch. Logs are sent with the logger
    as the instrumentation scope. Metric points the sender sent are forwarded,
    counters as delta monotonic sums, gauges and histograms. The points mcpd
    derives from spans are not sent: they repeat what the spans already say,
    and the destination would count them twice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spans are grouped by service into <code>resourceSpans</code>, the shape a
    receiver expects. Scores, feedback and aspect notes stay in mcpd.
  </p>

  <h2>Filters</h2>
  <p>
    <code>filter</code> narrows what one destination receives. Every list is
    optional, and an empty one lets everything through.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>projects</code>: only these projects.</li>
    <li><code>environments</code>: only these environments.</li>
    <li><code>kinds</code>: only these span kinds.</li>
    <li>
      <code>sample</code>: a fraction from 0 to 1. It is decided from the trace
      id, so a trace goes whole or not at all, and the logs in a trace follow
      the trace.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    What a filter holds back is counted in
    <code>mcpd_forward_filtered_total</code>, so a destination that receives
    less than you expect shows why.
  </p>

  <h2>Redaction</h2>
  <p>
    Forwarding happens after the intake pipeline, which redacts in place before
    anything is written. Whatever mcpd was told to hide never reaches a
    destination either.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>redact</code> goes further for one destination. It changes that
    destination's copy only, never what mcpd stores.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>drop_payloads: true</code> sends no span input or output and no log
      fields. The destination still gets timing, model, usage, cost and errors.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>key_contains</code> is a list of substrings. A key that contains
      one has its value replaced by <code>[redacted]</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So you can keep full payloads in your own store and send a vendor the shape
    of each run without its content.
  </p>

  {/* The diagram belongs here, not at the top. It draws the intake ending at
      Store, and this section is about what happens after Store, so the two read
      as one sequence rather than as a picture that stops where the subject
      begins. */}
  <PipelineDiagram class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>Local storage happens first</h2>
  <p>
    A batch is written to mcpd's own store before anything is forwarded. The
    copy is taken after the local write and handed to each destination's
    spool. A slow destination never adds its latency to the request that
    produced the span, and a destination that is down never turns into a
    rejected ingest.
  </p>

  <h3>The spool</h3>
  <p>
    Each destination has a spool directory, <code>&lt;spool-dir&gt;/forward-&lt;name&gt;/</code>.
    A copy is written to a temporary file, synced and renamed into place, with
    mode 0600, so a crash leaves a whole entry or none. The spool directory
    defaults to a <code>.spool</code> directory beside <code>--data</code>, such
    as <code>/data/mcpd.spool</code>, and is held in memory when
    <code>--data</code> is <code>:memory:</code>. <code>--spool-dir memory</code>
    keeps it in memory on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    A spool is bounded by <code>spool_max_bytes</code>, 256 MiB per destination
    unless you set it. When it is full, the new copy is dropped and counted in
    <code>mcpd_forward_spool_full_total</code>; the local write is unaffected. A
    backlog on disk survives a restart and is sent at start. On stop, a spool in
    memory gets one final attempt.
  </p>

  <h3>Retries</h3>
  <p>
    Two workers per destination send what is in the spool. A failed send is
    retried with exponential backoff and jitter: the wait starts at 0.5 seconds
    and doubles up to 60, and each wait is chosen at random in the upper half of
    that range. A <code>Retry-After</code> in seconds or as an HTTP date is
    honoured, up to 300 seconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Retried: 401, 403, 404, 408, 409, 425, 429, every 5xx, transport errors,
    and the gRPC codes <code>CANCELLED</code>, <code>DEADLINE_EXCEEDED</code>,
    <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>, <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>,
    <code>ABORTED</code>, <code>OUT_OF_RANGE</code>, <code>UNAVAILABLE</code>,
    <code>DATA_LOSS</code> and <code>UNAUTHENTICATED</code>. An expired key or a
    destination still being created therefore waits rather than losing data.
  </p>
  <p>
    Any other 4xx is final, because the batch will stay malformed for that
    destination. The entry is moved to <code>dead/</code> in the spool
    directory, the newest 100 are kept, and it is counted. An entry still
    undelivered after a day is moved to <code>dead/</code> too. Bodies over
    1 KiB are gzipped for OTLP/HTTP.
  </p>

  <h2>Watching it</h2>
  <p>
    Every metric carries the <code>destination</code> and <code>vendor</code>
    labels.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_sent_total</code></td><td>counter</td><td>Items the destination accepted</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_failed_attempts_total</code></td><td>counter</td><td>Attempts that failed and will be retried</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_dead_letter_total</code></td><td>counter</td><td>Items refused for good or older than a day, moved to <code>dead/</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_spool_full_total</code></td><td>counter</td><td>Items dropped because the spool was full</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_filtered_total</code></td><td>counter</td><td>Items the filter kept from the destination</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_spool_entries</code></td><td>gauge</td><td>Requests waiting in the spool</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_spool_bytes</code></td><td>gauge</td><td>Bytes the spool holds</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_up</code></td><td>gauge</td><td>1 when the last attempt succeeded or none has been made, 0 when it failed</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_last_success_timestamp_seconds</code></td><td>gauge</td><td>When the destination last accepted a request, in seconds since the epoch, or 0</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcpd_forward_dropped_spans_total</code></td><td>counter</td><td>Spans dead-lettered, dropped at a full spool, or dropped past the 64 copies in flight</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Alert on a growing <code>mcpd_forward_spool_bytes</code> and a stale
    <code>mcpd_forward_last_success_timestamp_seconds</code>: together they say
    a destination has stopped taking data while mcpd keeps it for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd doctor</code> reports one finding per destination. It sends an
    export that carries nothing, in the destination's own protocol, so the
    endpoint and the credential are proved without writing data. Helicone is
    skipped. It also reads the
    spool directory and reports its entries, bytes, the age of the oldest entry
    and the dead-letter count, and warns when there are dead letters or the
    oldest entry is over an hour old.
  </p>

  <h2>mcpd forwarding to mcpd</h2>
  <p>
    Because both ends speak the same protocol, a local mcpd can forward to a
    shared one. Developers keep a fast store on their own machine with full
    payloads, and the team's instance gets the same runs with its own retention
    and its own redaction rules applied on arrival. To put a collector in front
    of a daemon that stores nothing itself, use
    <a href="/docs/relay" class="link">the relay</a>.
  </p>

